#passage(("[Aisha] didn't leave us a book that has a list of why she's a Muslim. But we have reports, and by studying these reports, we can come to certain conclusions. Sometimes the reports are explicit. Sometimes you need to look deep into them to extract these reasons. So one of these reports: she says that when he was healthy, he used to say to me: the prophets would see their homes in paradise before they pass away. Before the prophet, peace be upon him, passed away, he had his head on her lap, and he fainted. He woke up, pointed upwards, and he said, \"Oh Allah, the highest companion,\" Aisha says. And that's what he seems to have been saying previously, when he was healthy. That was when he saw it.", "Before the prophet passed away, he calls Fatma to him, and he whispers something into Fatma's ear, his daughter, and she cries; he whispers something else, and she laughs. And after the prophet passes away, she said: \"The first time when I cried, it's because he told me that he's going to pass away soon. And the second time he said to me that you're going to be passing away soon after me. You're the first that's going to be passing away.\" And surely, six months after the prophet, peace be upon him, passes away, Fatima passed away.", "A short while before the prophet, peace be upon him, passed away as well, he calls [Uthman], and the prophet, peace be upon him, says to him: \"Allah may be adorning you with a shirt. If the hypocrites come to you and wanted you to take it off, then do not do so until you meet me.\" This could be anything. Aisha understood this after [Uthman] passed away: it was referring to those that came to him and tried to get him to step down from his position as the Khalifa. [Ibn Bashir], who's narrating this report, says something very interesting. He says: then what were you thinking? Where were you from this hadith? And Aisha says, \"Wallah, I just forgot.\"", "Aisha at first agreed with the complaints that the rebels came forth with. They seem to be fair. So why would Aisha implicate herself? There's no reason to. Aisha is not talking to a group of non-Muslims. Her audience, they are the Muslims. They're not going to get a boost of faith from this. They already believe in this. And yet she implicated herself by narrating this report.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood - A'isha”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R2n1dP6j9iI", ((1990, "33:10–34:14"), (2066, "34:26–35:21"), (2130, "35:30–36:35"), (2208, "36:48–37:35"),), id: 1211)

