#passage(("I've come to realize that this specific prophecy that most Muslims are familiar with is actually extremely underrated. Chapter 30 of the Quran: \"The Romans have been defeated in the nearest land, and after their defeat [they] will triumph in a few years.\"", "Many Muslims are aware that the Romans were at war with the Persians during the time of the Prophet, peace be upon him, but we aren't aware of the details, which is why many of us don't appreciate this as much as we should. Because what many people think is: these are the two biggest empires at the time, you have a 50-50 chance of guessing who would be victorious. Fair enough, but that's only if you don't know any of the details.", "Heraclius, who was in Carthage, came into power when he was sent by his father, a general, to eliminate the emperor [Phocas]. Apparently [Phocas] wasn't too popular, because he overthrew emperor Maurice before him. Unfortunately for [Heraclius], he didn't have full control over the legions, since some were loyal to [Phocas]. [Khosrow], the emperor of Persia, made use of the situation by going to war with the Romans after the first year of Heraclius's rule.", "Theophanies states the following: \"In May the Persians campaigned against Syria, took Apamaya and Edessa and advanced as far as Antioch. The Romans met them, fought and were beaten. The whole Roman army was destroyed, so that very few men got away.\" In the next year the Persians conquered Caesarea, and a couple of years later they captured Damascus. Theophanies states: \"The emperor Heraclius sent envoys to [Khosrow] to stop the merciless bloodshed, set the payment of tribute and obtained treaties, but [Khosrow] sent the ambassadors away unsuccessful.\" In the next year the Persians took Jordan and Palestine.", "Heraclius, realizing that they did not have much of a chance, sent a written dispatch that included the following, as we find in the [Chronicon Paschale]: \"We beg of your clemency to consider Heraclius, our most pious emperor, as a true son, one who is eager to perform the service of your serenity in all things. After the death of the usurper, our emperor wished to take his relatives and to return to his father in Africa, after urging us to elect the man we wanted as emperor.\" In other words, Heracles is saying: I didn't even want to be emperor, please leave me alone; if I could, I would have simply went back to Africa. And do you know how [Khosrow] replied? In the very next year he captured all of Africa except for Carthage.", "I was just referring to the Persian attacks; they weren't the only ones picking off the Roman territory. Theophanies writes about the end of the decade: \"And this year the Avars attacked Thrace.\" The Pannonian Avars were considered nomadic barbarians that settled in East Europe. So how did Heraclius deal with this other threat? Theophanies continues by saying: \"Heracles sent envoys to them to ask for peace, and when the Kagan agreed to this, the emperor went outside the long walls with the entire imperial bodyguard. He promised the Kagan many great gifts and got pledges from him that they would make peace with each other. But the barbarian set aside his agreements and oaths, suddenly and treacherously advancing against the emperor. Heracleus was thunderstruck at this unexpected affair and fled to the city. The barbarian captured the imperial great and bodyguard and whatever else he could reach, then withdrew, plundering many villages in Thrace.\"", "According to [Nikephoros], Heracles, out of desperation for peace, gave the Avars his son as a hostage. John was a son of a concubine, but he was Heracles' son nonetheless.", "In the year 621 the verses were revealed. This prophecy was made at the time in which things were looking up for Muslims. The boycott against the Prophet, peace be upon him, had ended; the people in Medina started to accept Islam. After a decade of preaching Islam and gaining the hearts of the people, the Prophet, peace be upon him, risked everything by making this prophecy. The chapter was recited publicly, as the Quran is a living text, and the disbelievers listen closely, even placing bets against the Muslims that the Romans would not attain victory within the time frame.", "Things weren't getting much better in the following years. In fact, in the year 626 Constantinople was besieged by both the Avars and the Persians. The Avars' army alone was around 80,000 men; however, the Romans were able to survive the siege. A year and a half later, Heracles was able to strike a deal with the Persian general Shahad Baraz. Siroy, [Khosrow's] eldest son, betrays his father, executes him, and then makes peace with the Byzantines out of desperation. Heracles returns to Constantinople, and the war is over in the year 628. In seven years the prophecy comes to pass, and the Romans, who seem to have no hope of winning, turn things around miraculously and win the war.", "Niyar bin Mukhram al-Islami comments that when this occurred many people embraced Islam. In regards to the time of this prophecy, this prophecy comes to pass seven years after it was initially revealed, in Hudaibiyah. Qatada also affirms that this occurs at Houdaibia. Other companions like [Ibn Abbas] also testify in separate reports that this prophecy came to pass.",), "Farid Responds, “The Riskiest Prophecy by the Prophet (peace be upon him)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OitlzW6Iq4M", ((5, "0:05–0:19"), (21, "0:21–0:48"), (48, "0:48–1:15"), (78, "1:18–2:01"), (121, "2:01–2:52"), (176, "2:56–4:06"), (248, "4:08–4:20"), (260, "4:20–4:57"), (297, "4:57–5:50"), (353, "5:53–6:19"),), id: 1212)
// U FR212:1-59 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("The words used for \"a few years\" in Arabic, [bid'], means three to nine years. Initially gambling was permissible in Islam, and when the polytheists had heard of these verses, they challenged [Abu Bakr] to a wager in which they agreed to a time limit of five years. However, the Byzantines were not able to defeat the Persians within five years. So the Muslims, when they returned to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and asked him how come the Byzantines weren't able to defeat the Persians within five years, he said: why didn't you agree to anything under 10? So he doubled down on the prophecy, instead of taking it back or switching it up. Eventually, within that time limit, within those nine years, the Byzantines did defeat the Persians.", "The riskiest type of prophecy to make are the ones that are limited to a specific time, because if you're unable to see that prophecy through, or for that prophecy to come to pass within that time limit, you risk losing everything. There's nothing to gain from making a time limited prophecy. However, it was due to the Prophet, peace be upon him, confidence in Allah and in the Quran that he was able to make this prophecy, and naturally it came to pass.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood 9: \"The Byzantines will be Victorious\"”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_y9urpx3T1Y", ((22, "0:22–1:16"), (87, "1:27–1:58"),), id: 1213)
