#passage(("You had the two major empires which had more than half of the planet: the Roman and Persian empire, the Byzantine and the Sasanian empire. They were fighting each other. This is around the time of the prophet, peace be upon him, from 618 AD. And you have these battles taking place, and the Romans have been defeated, and the Persians have completely annihilated them. They took over Damascus, they took over Antioch, they took over Jerusalem. The Roman Empire was a Christian Empire that time, the time of the prophet, because Constantine adopted Christianity in the 4th century. So, Jerusalem was the heart of the Empire. So, the Persians took over Jerusalem. Historians at that time were saying that's the end of the Roman Empire.", "Then the Quran comes and makes a definitive claim that nobody would make. Because if you want to make a claim when you don't know the truth, you'll make the easy claim. You will say the Persians will destroy the Romans; they're already destroying them. No, but the Quran comes and says: no, the Romans have been defeated, in chapter 30, but they are going to defeat the Persians. And it gives you a specific time frame, uses the word fi bid'i sinin.", "Some people might sometimes ask, why does it say three to nine? It doesn't say three to nine in numbers. It just uses a word in the Arabic language which was bid'i, which means three to nine. It's like saying \"in a few years\". Few is not a number, but it ranges from a specific set of numbers. You can't say few is 500. That's not few.", "So, the Quran gives you a specific framework. If I am a liar, even if I want to make that claim, I'm going to say the Romans are going to bounce back one day and defeat the Persians, because I'm worried. I'm not going to put a time frame if I'm a liar, especially not a three to nine years, which is very very short period of time for an empire to revive itself and defeat another empire when it's on the brink of collapse.", "So why does the Quran say from three to nine years? Because if you look, the defeats were happening year after year. Defeats were not like one defeat and that was it. No, they had multitude of battles. And you can even say some historians arguing when exactly is the year of defeat: was it after six years, seven years, eight years, or nine years? But you will see all of these defeats are within the time frame of the Quran. And that's why the Quran is using that word. So he's being comprehensive of the subjective interpretations of defeat as well.", "There's another prophecy, because it says the believers will rejoice that day. And with that same day that the Romans were defeated, the believers had a battle, and they won the battle, and they were rejoicing.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Muslim Surprises Intelligent Lady With Undeniable Evidence For Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qzJXd-FotIo", ((1136, "18:56–20:13"), (1213, "20:13–20:35"), (1241, "20:41–20:57"), (1262, "21:02–21:25"), (1287, "21:27–21:58"), (1406, "23:26–23:36"),), id: 1217)
