#passage(("The battle of Badr includes some really fascinating signs for the [prophethood] of Rasulallah. The Muslims at the time were in Medina, and Abu Sufyan, one of the chiefs of disbelievers of Quraysh, was returning from [Sham]. He had some goods with him, and he was with 70 of his men for protection. The Muslims decided to make a move to intercept them. When the Meccans heard of this, they decided to intercept the Muslims. At this point, Allah the Almighty reveals to the Prophet, peace be upon him, that the Muslims will be successful against one of these two groups; however, he didn't specify which. The Muslims were naturally hoping for a victory against Abu Sufyan and his men, instead of going into an all-out war with Quraish: \"Remember, O believers, when Allah promised you one of the two groups, that it would be yours, and you wish that the unarmed one would be yours, but Allah intended to establish the truth by His words and to eliminate the disbelievers.\"", "Naturally, the Muslims weren't hoping for a major clash at the time. They only had 300 something infantry men, with two knights with them. However, when they realized that they would be clashing and that there would be an all-out war, their reactions changed: \"It is just as when your Lord brought you out of your home in truth, while indeed a party among the believers were unwilling, arguing with you concerning the truth after it had become clear, as if they were being driven toward death while they were looking on.\" The disbelievers, on the other hand, were very well equipped, overconfident, and outnumbered the Muslims three to one. The Quran records their reactions as well: \"And do not be like those who came forth from their homes insolently and to be seen by people, and avert them from the way of Allah. And Allah is encompassing of what they do.\"", "In a prophetic hadith, Ibn Abbas narrates the following: on the day of the battle of Beder, the Prophet said: \"O Allah, I appeal to You to fulfill Your covenant and promise. O Allah, if Your will is that none should worship You, then give victory to the pagans.\" The reason that he said this is because if the Muslims lost this battle, it would have been the end of Islam. They were going up against the kuffar three to one, and it would be really likely that the Prophet, peace be upon him, would be killed. The Prophet, peace be upon him, then recited: \"Their assembly will be defeated, and they will turn their backs and retreats.\"", "This was a highly unlikely prophecy. The Muslims were severely outnumbered, three to one; they were anxious, and their opponents were over confident; and yet they decimated their enemies, killing 70 and capturing 70 as prisoners of war, all while suffering from minimal losses.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood 19: Battle of Badr”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RFOMiBrSv4w", ((1, "0:01–1:06"), (67, "1:07–2:08"), (128, "2:08–2:47"), (175, "2:55–3:13"),), id: 1230)
