#passage(("The Prophet gives an example of a prophecy that is individual bound. There was a person called Suraqa ibn Malik. He was following the Prophet, peace be upon him. He was sent by the pagan Arabs to kill the Prophet when he left Mecca, because they were persecuting him. So he was sent as a bounty hunter to capture the Prophet. And when he was trying to kill him, his horse sank in the sand. He realised that there's something about this man. And then the Prophetﷺ told him a prophecy. He said: you, Suraqa, will hold the bracelets of the Persian king.", "You know who the Persian king was? He was the most powerful man on earth at that time, because the Persian empire was, you can say, the most powerful empire at the time, and he was the king of the Persian empire. The Prophet himself was leaving Mecca, because they were plotting to kill him, and he had only one Companion with him. And now he's telling him: you, individual, I'm telling you: you see the king, the most powerful man on earth? You are going to hold his bracelets. Anyone would look at this and say: you wouldn't even know if the Prophet is going to survive the next day or not. He's already leaving, and this man is sent to kill him.", "So the Prophet lived his life. Then Muslims increased in number, and they defeated the pagan Arabs. This individual became Muslim, Suraqa, later. He could have died any day before he wore the bracelets: he could have died of disease, he could have been assassinated; there's so many possibilities. He fought many battles with Muslims. The Prophet passed away; the first caliphate of Islam, Abu Bakr, passed away; and then, at the time of the second caliphate of Islam, 'Umar ibn alKhattab, he overcame the Persian empire, and the bracelets of the Persian king were given to that man, Suraqa, that individual that, years and years, tens of years before, was sent to kill the Prophet. He held the bracelets of the most powerful man on earth at that time.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Muslim Tactically Turns The Tables On A Christian! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=miaGqA6AFCs", ((635, "10:35–11:02"), (669, "11:09–11:47"), (711, "11:51–12:36"),), id: 1234)
