#passage(("When the Prophet, peace be upon him, moved to Medina, he was surrounded by enemies: hypocrites from within, as well as the Jews of Medina. After [Banu Nadir], a Jewish tribe, betrayed their alliance with the Muslims, there was a conversation that occurred between them and the hypocrites of Medina: \"Have you not considered those who practice hypocrisy, saying to their brothers who have disbelieved among the People of the Scripture: if you are expelled, we will surely leave with you, and we will not obey, in regard to you, anyone ever; and if you are fought, we will surely aid you. But Allah testifies that they are liars.\" This is not supposed to be a conversation that is occurring publicly; it's a secret conversation that occurred. [Allah] is exposing them for this.", "A prophecy occurs in the very next verse: \"If they are expelled, they will not leave with them, and if they are fought, they will not aid them. And even if they should aid them, they will surely turn their backs; then thereafter they will not be aided.\" And that is exactly what occurred: when Beni Navid were expelled, the disbelievers did not leave with them, despite the fact that the Prophet, peace be upon him, recited these verses aloud publicly. By doing this, he was exposing the hypocrites and challenging them to fulfill their words. And that is a proof of prophethood.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood 28: Challenge to Hypocrites”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UDZJJheu8SA", ((7, "0:07–1:06"), (66, "1:06–1:50"),), id: 1238)
// U FR197:1-20 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("We find in the Quran the following verses: \"They will not harm you except for some annoyance. And if they fight you, they will show you their backs. Then they will not be aided.\" Al-Qadi Abd al-Jabbar and exegesis of the Quran identify this as a very clear prophecy that came to pass. The Muslims that were in Medina at the time were surrounded by the Jewish tribes Bani Qaynuqa, Bani Nadir, and Bani Qurayza. And not too far away, you even had the Jews of Khaybar. Now, the verse doesn't simply predict that the Muslims will be victorious against them, but it goes as far to say that they will not be harmed in any meaningful way. Not only that, the Jews there at the time were very well equipped and were actually quite dangerous.", "Al-Qadi Abd al-Jabbar also points out that revealing these verses publicly doesn't really fit in with actions of man. If a king feels negatively towards some tribes that are living on his land, the last thing that he would want to do is reveal the animosity that he feels towards them, because that would simply bring hostility towards him. Furthermore, how could he be certain that they would not be receiving harm from the Jews? One could only make a prediction like this if they had complete certainty. And in this case, that certainty came in the form of revelation.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood 24: Jews of Hijaz”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vGK2bTjYXmg", ((6, "0:06–0:59"), (59, "0:59–1:39"),), id: 1239)
// U FR014:44-87 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("When the battle ends, and when the Ahzab left Medina and returned, the prophet, peace be upon him, said something very important. We find in Sahih al-Bukhari hadith number 4110, at least this is in Arabic numbering, that Abu Ishaq as-Sabi'i narrates from Sulayman bin Surad al-Khuza'i. Khuza'a, they were the allies of the Muslims. Sulayman says that he heard the prophet, peace be upon him, said after the Ahzab, after Ghatafan and Quraysh returned: \"Now, Nazghuhum, we will attack them, and they will not attack us. And we will march towards them.\"", "The prophet, peace be upon him, saying that is quite fascinating, because the Muslims only survived due to Allah Subhana Wa Ta'ala's wind. So, you do not expect the prophet, peace be upon him, to make a prophecy so soon after, immediately after. Now he's saying: \"We're going to attack them, and they will not attack us.\" So, the Muslims attacking them: you can say that's a self-fulfilling prophecy. You can say someone's saying \"I'm going to attack them\", and then they actually raise an army, and they attack them. You could say self-fulfilling. \"They will not attack us\": how do you know that they're not going to attack you? The only reason they weren't able to is because of the wind. Why don't they come back a few months later? Why don't they come back a year later? Prophet from God. That's why. That's how he knows.", "And subhanallah, this is exactly what happens. Not too long after the Khandaq, you have the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah, and then soon after that, you have the conquest of Mecca. Quraysh never attacked the Muslims in Medina ever again. And if you think about it, it's so unlikely for that to happen. The Muslims and Quraysh going to war with one another was something that was happening almost on a yearly basis. Why would they not attack again? Did the Muslims all of a sudden build a major army? No, the war just ended. They barely made it to life. There is no reason for Quraysh to not attack again. However, we're dealing with a prophet from Allah subhana wa ta'ala, and he has that knowledge that was just given to him from above.",), "Farid Responds, “Underappreciated Prophecy Made in 627 CE”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9VrIY2l0P14", ((243, "4:03–4:51"), (295, "4:55–5:53"), (355, "5:55–6:44"),), id: 1240)
// R hadith_bukhari_4110.json
#refbox("Reference text: sunnah.com, Sahih al-Bukhari 4110", (("", "Narrated Sulaiman bin Surd:"), ("", "When the clans were driven away, I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) saying, \"From now onwards we will go to attack them (i.e. the infidels) and they will not come to attack us, but we will go to them.\""),))
// U FR205:1-45 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("The Prophet, peace be upon him, was forced to leave Makkah because of the oppression that he faced, and then these verses were revealed: \"Indeed, He who imposed upon you the Quran will take you back to a place of return.\" The place of return mentioned in the verse is ma'ad, which Ibn Abbas refers to as Makkah: \"When We made the House a place of return for the people and a place of security.\" And I'd personally like to add another verse of my own to give this more clarity: \"Certainly Allah showed to His Messenger the vision in truth: you will surely enter al-Masid al-Haram, if Allah wills, in safety, with your heads shaved and hair shortened, not fearing anyone. He knew what you did not know and has arranged before that a conquest near at hand.\"", "The Prophet was oppressed in Mecca for 13 years, and at that time it was not a prophecy that seemed likely to come to pass. However, it was a prophecy that was made, and a few years before his death, not only did he return to Mecca, but he actually conquered Mecca, performed umrah, performed the pilgrimage. Now that is a prophecy that came to pass.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood 16: Prophecy of Return to Makkah”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MpgTEz6ogZg", ((12, "0:12–1:11"), (74, "1:14–1:38"),), id: 1241)
// U FR200:1-57 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("This one is a prophecy from the Quran: \"Oh you who believed, whoever of you should revert from his religion, Allah will bring forth in place of them a people who will love and who will love Him, who are humble toward the believers, strong against the disbelievers; they strive in the cause of Allah and do not fear the blame of a critic.\"", "Firstly, notice that the verse says \"whoever of you should revert from his religion\". It says here minkum, \"from among you\". It doesn't say \"whoever reverts from his religion\" in general; it's specifically referring to the people around the Prophet, peace be upon him. Now, this is a prophecy that occurred when the Prophet, peace be upon him, passed away: a lot of the tribes in the Arabian peninsula apostated from Islam. The first part of the prophecy is that there will be people that were Muslims at the time that will leave Islam. And the second part of the prophecy is that Allah will bring Muslims to fight those that had left Islam, and that occurred when Abu Bakr fought the apostates during the Riddha wars.", "Now, there are a number of ways in which this prophecy could have failed. One is that perhaps no one would have left Islam; they would have just stayed as Muslims after the Prophet passed away. It was also possible that when the apostasy actually occurred, no one would have stepped up against them. In a relatively short time, Abu Bakr successfully subjugated the apostates, and the peninsula came under the rule of Islam once again. And that together is a clear proof of prophethood.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood 21: Ridda Wars”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yfylQut-Ae8", ((6, "0:06–0:27"), (29, "0:29–1:24"), (84, "1:24–1:53"),), id: 1242)
// U FR005:83-90 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("Anas narrated that Umm Sulaim said to the prophet, peace be upon him: \"Anas is your servant.\" The prophet said: \"Oh Allah, increase his wealth and offspring, and bless for him whatever you give him.\" And Anas actually did become one of the richest from among the Ansar in Medina. And not only that, but he lived for a very long time, and he was blessed with many children and grandchildren, up to 100, perhaps over 100 descendants.",), "Farid Responds, “Test your Knowledge - Ten Hadiths”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LOCLrBxohGM", ((429, "7:09–7:40"),), id: 1243)

