#passage(("I posed a question to Samuel, asking him about his criteria for an authentic historical report. I was pleasantly surprised to find David attempting to answer this. We have three things: the earlier the better, multiple, and independent.", "Firstly, just because a report is earlier doesn't mean that it's authentic. Let's say you have two sources, a fifth century source and a third Century Source. Apparently the earlier the better; that doesn't mean that the third Century source is authentic. You're still Disconnected by three centuries. In any case, that's not even a condition. Yes, it's something that you prefer.", "In terms of the report being in multiple sources: well, is that a condition as well? What if two sources are quoting the same Source? What if the Second Source is quoting the First Source? Just because you have multiple sources doesn't mean that the information is authentic. And I don't even think David believes in this, because of what he says next: multiple sources aren't necessary in order to affirm that a miracle happened, or that a historical event occurred. So you should have an objective criteria. This should be evidence that's binding upon all the world; it should not be something that's tied to Faith.", "Let's put this stuff to the test. [Nabil], who passes away in the year 1892, he was a Baha'i historian, and he wrote about the [Bab]. The [Bab], by the way, passed away in the year 1850. So El Nabil used to report that [the Bab] had the ability to teleport, and teleported out of his own imprisonment. Do you believe this, David? Because as a contemporary, it's a contemporaneous source, and remember, the earlier the better.",), "Farid Responds, “Did Muhammad ﷺ Perform Miracles? [Response to David Wood and Apostate Prophet]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zgHtmG55-w0", ((785, "13:05–14:24"), (868, "14:28–14:51"), (898, "14:58–16:19"), (979, "16:19–16:53"),), id: 1314)
