#passage(("It is the biggest miracle you can have. And it's objective; there's a criteria. It's telling you what you need to do. We have the pre-Islamic poetry, we have the language of the Arabs, and we have dictionaries. So we know that these constructs were not used before. We know this is not how the Arabs spoke. So when I say this is new, there is evidence, because we have the pre-Islamic poetry.", "You cannot know, because the only Miracle that is left is the Miracle of the last Prophet, because the last Prophet is the Prophet that is for everyone, while the other prophets were only for their people. So their Miracles were not intended for everyone to begin with. The Miracles of Musa عليه السلام was for the children of Israel. It was not for you to see anyways. Those Miracles were not for us, so it would make sense that we cannot verify them anyways, because they are not for us.", "That's why the poet, he said: جاءَ النبِيّونَ بِالآياتِ فَانْصَرَمَتْ وَجِئتَنا بِحَكيمٍ غَيرِ مُنصَرِمِ. The Prophets, they came with the signs, Miracles, so they went away. And you came with حكيم, a miraculous book, and wise book, a constructed book that is never going to end. آياتُهُ كُلَّما طالَ المَدى جُدُدٌ زيَّنَهُنَّ جَلالُ العِتقِ وَالقِدَمِ: Verses that the more time passes, the more you'd see miracles and beauty in it. That's the poet talking about the Quran. The Prophets, their miracles are gone now. No one can see them. But how can we know that they are there, is by knowing that the Quran is from God. So once I've proved that the Quran is from God, by default whatever it says is true, because it's from God, and God knows what happened in History.", "The Prophet صلى الله عليه وسلم, there's so many miracles recorded of him doing physical miracles. For example, the very common ones are known: the Prophet عليه الصلاة والسلام, they didn't have water, and he brought a small amount of water, and water started pouring out of his fingers. There's thousands of people who witnessed this event, because it was in a battle and he had thousands of people. There was food: there wasn't enough bread. The Prophet baked it, and it fed a whole army of people, while it was a little bread and meat, barely enough for one family.", "And the idea of the Prophet splitting the moon: what happened is the Prophet did a miracle for the disbelievers of Quraysh, and then Allah split the moon into two parts, and then Allah re-joined the moon. And this miracle was witnessed by some of the disbelievers and a lot of the believers, and it's recorded by many of the Companions, and it's recorded even by the testimony of some of the disbelievers. There is a record of an Indian king. He travelled to the Prophet, and he became a Muslim after this event took place, and he said he saw it. If you search certain India's Libraries, they will tell you that this happened. But this type of miracles are for the time, for the people.", "These are just some. For example, the Prophet عليه الصلاة والسلام had the revelation descending upon him in different times. Aisha رضي الله عنها, she said, the Prophet عليه الصلاة والسلام had the revelation coming on him in winter, and he used to sweat. Do you know the winter in the desert, how it is? We've not seen winter, because we've got buildings or walls protecting us, and heat, and all of these cars and exhaustion. You don't know really cold. Sit in the desert where there's no barriers: it's proper cold. She said, in the most cold day, he used to sweat when the revelation used to come on him. When the revelation came to him, and his leg was on the leg of another companion, he said his leg almost broke from the weight of the revelation that came.", "Miracles that happened in the life of the Prophet عليه الصلاة والسلام are so many. Full books written on that. So the claim that \"Oh, the prophet didn't have these things\": a lot of Christians say that, but they have no clue about what happened.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Arab Christian Introduced To The Challenge Of The Quran! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n-flvFktgzU", ((588, "9:48–10:31"), (631, "10:31–11:47"), (713, "11:53–12:39"), (764, "12:44–13:12"), (796, "13:16–14:16"), (870, "14:30–15:07"), (915, "15:15–15:25"),), id: 1317)
