#passage(("On one occasion when the prayer was at hand, everyone was looking for water for wudu, but no one could find any. Then the messenger of Allah brought some water in a vessel. He put his hand into the vessel, and then he told them all to do [wudu] from it. Anas added: I saw water coming out from his fingers. Then all of them, to the last man, did.", "That's the claim, and that's something that we have in a book which is a very early work. The question is, of course: is this report correctly attributed to Malik? And there's absolutely no reason to doubt this attribution, because the major Renditions of Malik's book ([Laythi's] rendition is the most popular rendition), they all attribute this book to Malik. Similarly, you have these external sources: they both narrate this Hadith from Malik; there's like one person between them and Malik. So we know that this book, quite early, included this report. Malik's narrating this Hadith, so there's only one person between him and the eyewitness of this report.", "Is this report from [Anas] only narrated by [Ishaq]? No, it's narrated by four more of his students. You have these reports coming in through multiple sources; you have multiple early sources attributing these reports to these different students.", "Now, the only question that could come to mind is: why should we trust [Anas]? Even though there are five people narrating this Hadith, maybe [Anas] made it all up. Well, the problem with that is you have nine Companions of the Prophet peace be upon him all saying that he caused water to multiply. There may be more; I'm mainly relying on this book for these reports, which is an authentic collection of narrations about the proofs of prophethood. You have all these different people, nine different people, all mentioning different times, in different places: multiple incidents in which water is being caused to multiply.", "These reports are being narrated in different times. [Mu'adh] is really early: he dies like seven years after the prophet peace be upon him. So in the few years after the death of the Prophet peace be upon him, this report is initially circulated. So it doesn't come like 90 years later, in a source which we don't have from that time, attributed anonymously to different people. The latest companion, 80 years later, is the companion of the Prophet that lived for that long, 80 years apart from the prophet peace be upon him, but he's an eyewitness.", "These companions, they're in different regions: some Medina, some in [Basra], Kufa, etc. So they couldn't collaborate to make up these stories. And of course there are differences between them as well: they're not all referring to the same incident. One actually says it's happening in Medina; one's happening in [Hudaybiyyah]. So these mostly don't seem to be referring to the same exact incident.",), "Farid Responds, “Miracles of Jesus vs Muhammad (peace be upon them)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sgzDSausNlY", ((551, "9:11–9:37"), (579, "9:39–11:06"), (679, "11:19–12:21"), (745, "12:25–13:45"), (830, "13:50–15:02"), (905, "15:05–15:36"),), id: 1319)
// U FR199:1-42 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("\"Remember when he overwhelmed you with drowsiness, giving security from him.\" \"Then after distress he sent down upon you security in the form of drowsiness, overcoming [a faction] of you.\" So Allah the almighty is actually saying that he brought down upon the Muslims during war a form of drowsiness, in order to calm their nerves. Now, don't worry: the drowsiness didn't prevent the Muslims from defeating the disbelievers [in] the battle of Badr. After the surprise attack against the Muslims, it helped them calm their nerves and help them survive the battle. Abu Talha said: \"I raised my head to look around on the day, and there was not one of them that day except that he was swaying under his shield due to drowsiness.\"", "Of course, this is a supernatural event that did actually occur. If it didn't occur, then the prophet peace be upon him wouldn't be reciting these verses publicly, and doing that would put his whole case for prophethood at jeopardy.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood 22: Drowsiness in Battle”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y6eLwW54TmQ", ((20, "0:20–1:14"), (77, "1:17–1:32"),), id: 1320)
// U FR126:103-151 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("Verses in the Quran speak of signs clearly. Take, for example, a wind that saved the Muslims from being absolutely annihilated by multiple Arab tribes. The Muslims were besieged in Medina by ten thousand of their enemies, outnumbering the Muslims, whose total forces amounted to around 3,000. And they were also going to be attacked from behind. In any case, out of nowhere, a fierce wind [routs] the alliance and prevents them from annihilating the Muslims, causing them to return to their lands.", "A miracle can be the result of the strange timing of natural elements. For example, we return to First Samuel 7: we find that the Lord sent lightning and thunder against the Philistines, which threw them into a panic, turning the tides of War. The prophet Samuel of course recognized this as a miracle from the Lord. So this miracle was a sign for the prophet Samuel. He didn't need to wave his hands into the sky; he didn't need to toss a staff towards the heavens. Everyone realized this, and he confirmed it as such, and the Bible confirmed it as such. Similarly, the wind was a sign for the prophet peace be upon him.", "Once again, the verse: \"O Believers, remember Allah's favor upon you when enemy forces came to besiege you in Medina, so we sent against them a bitter wind and forces you could not see, and Allah is all seeing of what you do. Remember when they came at you from east and west, when your eyes grew wild in horror and your hearts jumped into your throats, and you entertained conflicting thoughts about Allah.\" And all of that was Gone with the coming of a wind.",), "Farid Responds, “Did Prophet Muhammad ﷺ Perform Miracles? [Response Video]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qC6saY7PZ6Y", ((247, "4:07–4:53"), (293, "4:53–5:33"), (333, "5:33–6:03"),), id: 1321)
// U FR250:407-446 | Farid Responds | 4
#passage(("There are some matters that some will see as supernatural, and some will see as maybe these are coincidences. So for example, in one night: \"I wish I had one of my good companions to come and to guard me.\" And they hear a sound outside the door: \"What are you doing at this time?\" And he says, \"I came to guard you.\" Maybe it's a coincidence, but maybe there was something more. Maybe Aisha saw this as proof ([Bukhari], Muslim).", "When Aisha was about to wash the prophet peace be upon him, the people in the room had a difference of opinion: should we wash him with his clothes on, or should we wash him with his clothes off? And then they all started to feel a bit drowsy, and they started to nod off. And then they heard a voice from the corner, or slightly outside, saying, \"Wash him with his clothes on.\" And then they just did that. They realized that there's something special going on (Muslim).", "One of my favorite examples: there's a man at the door, and he says, \"Did you put your weapons down?\" He says, \"Yeah, we're done.\" And he says, \"Get your weapons with you.\" Now, who's this guy who's giving orders? This man comes and gives the commander-in-chief, the ruler of Medina, orders in regards to what we're going to do next: we're going to have another battle or something right now. Get your weapons. We're going now. Aisha understands that this is [Jibril] in the form of a man. Is this supernatural? It's not supernatural. She just saw a man ordering the prophet peace be upon him. But Aisha understood, when she saw this, that all this stuff that's happening, everything that's going on, is not from my husband. There's someone else that's pulling the strings, and it's someone who I don't really recognize by name. I don't know who this guy is, but I know he's [Jibril]. Or that's her understanding. This is [Sahih] Muslim.", "This same man one day told the prophet peace be upon him that he wanted to meet up with him, and the prophet peace be upon him sat down waiting for him, waited for a long time, and he was late. Now, angels don't usually come late. The prophet sallallaihi wasallam was confused. He went outside, and he saw him standing outside, and the man said, \"We don't enter houses that have dogs or have pictures hung up.\" Now, I don't think that this man was afraid of dogs. I think that there is a deeper meaning in this: this was [Jibril], and due to matters that have to do with spirituality, he does not enter a house with dogs or pictures. And surely enough, there was a puppy under the bed at the time. [Sahih] Muslim.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood - A'isha”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R2n1dP6j9iI", ((2347, "39:07–39:48"), (2397, "39:57–40:40"), (2443, "40:43–42:10"), (2534, "42:14–43:19"),), id: 1322)

