#passage(("Abu Jabbar makes a very interesting claim. He states that the prophet, peace be upon him, was extremely eager for people to follow him and his message. That being said, why would he make a claim like that? Why would he claim that the moon split? Wouldn't making a claim like that be the fastest way for people to [reject] your claim of prophethood?", "Now imagine you going up to the people of your town and saying to them: I saw the moon split yesterday, and now it's back. Now, if you were someone who claimed prophethood, wouldn't everyone just reject you? Who would believe you if you said this? And why didn't Islam absolutely collapse when that happened? Why is it that more people ended up embracing Islam after this claim? Also keep in mind that this is something that occurred when Muslims were a minority in Makkah. If this is something that never happened, Islam should have collapsed immediately.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood 8: The Moon Splitting”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iSD5uV9J_RM", ((107, "1:47–2:08"), (130, "2:10–2:55"),), id: 1323)
// U FR391:1-42 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("The [Quran] affirms quite clearly that the disbelievers did not reject that event but rather explain it off as magic. It is important to be aware that the Prophet recited these verses in public continuously for years. He recited that the disbelievers saw the moon splitting and shrugged it off. If no such thing occurred, then he would have been called out for it. It wasn't only the Muslims that saw the moon split, but more importantly [the disbelievers] affirmed that it split.",), "Farid Responds, “[DEBUNKED] The Moon Splitting Fairy Tale - Apostate Prophet”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5SA3ws1dKwo", ((4, "0:04–1:46"),), id: 1324)
