#passage(("The polytheists of Makkah asked the prophet for a sign, and then the moon started to split. He then said to them: bear witness.", "I tend to argue that it was due to the lack of documentation that we do not have non-Islamic sources speaking about the moon splitting. A completely different theory argues that this was a miracle specifically for the people of Makkah and not for anyone else. The reasoning behind this is that if the moon was seen splitting in different regions of the world, it would be used by false claimants to push their own agendas. False prophets in different lands would claim that the moon split for them. And of course, imagine someone in Rome or someone in China seeing the moon split: how would they even tie that to the prophet, peace be upon him? Now, the prevention of people from witnessing the moon splitting in different regions of the world could have occurred through natural means like the weather, perhaps even some other supernatural means.", "Some seem to believe that there was some sort of 7th century manuscript about an Indian king witnessing the moon splitting. However, that is an unreliable manuscript. It was written in Arabic, it's quite late, it contains anachronisms. So no, that wouldn't be good evidence of that happening.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood 8: The Moon Splitting”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iSD5uV9J_RM", ((10, "0:10–0:18"), (27, "0:27–1:24"), (84, "1:24–1:45"),), id: 1332)
// U FR391:55-88 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("One of the reasons why we do not have heaps of documented evidence was because most people were asleep at night. Remember, this is 7th century. More importantly, there is no reason to assume that the split occurred for a long duration of time, so even if one was awake at the time, it is not likely that one would have noticed the moon splitting. Furthermore, even if you did see the moon split for a short period, you would most likely doubt yourself after seeing it returned to its original state. Going around telling other townfolk that you saw the moon split would pretty much cause them to doubt your sanity. If you were someone [that] documented events, you wouldn't believe who ever told you that [it split] in the first place.", "If by chance you were someone that did witness the moon splitting and documented, then, like most ancient documents, yours may have been lost due to the passage of time. Or maybe not; maybe such documentation has survived, but we simply have not heard of it yet. So is the absence of evidence evidence of absence? If so, then the Great Wall of China didn't exist during the time of Marco Polo, since he didn't make mention of it in his writings.",), "Farid Responds, “[DEBUNKED] The Moon Splitting Fairy Tale - Apostate Prophet”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5SA3ws1dKwo", ((139, "2:19–2:59"), (179, "2:59–3:26"),), id: 1333)
// U FR236:51-188 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("I listed several things that need to occur for you to have documentation of the moon splitting. One, you need to be awake. Two, you need to be outside looking at it when that's happening. Three, you need to believe what you're seeing. Four, you need to actually write it down. And if it's written down, and if it's not lost, then we have it. And the issue is, so much of what was written from the 7th century is lost. They would have made a record of this. People wouldn't have just ignored this, like: \"Oh look, the moon is split. Should we record this? Should we write this down? Nah, forget about it, just ignore it, not important.\" [Ridvan] is making it seem as if I'm arguing that this was never documented. Not once does he quote me saying that much of what was documented was lost.", "So what if there were libraries? Most of the information from those eras haven't even reached us. You don't have to take my word for it; at least listen to David Wood. The second emperor of Rome, who ruled for two decades, the most powerful man in the world at the time: you only have five sources even mentioning him, and you're expecting libraries of books that mention the moon splitting, even though it probably split for just a few seconds, a minute, who knows.",), "Farid Responds, “Apostate Prophet FINALLY Responded to Me!! (ft. David Wood)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h7J-0jpf3KI", ((190, "3:10–4:01"), (241, "4:01–6:29"),), id: 1334)
// U FR236:199-249 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("David believes the sun stopped in the sky to allow Israel to slaughter the enemies, according to the Bible, and no one else saw it: \"On the day the Lord gave the Amorites over to Israel, Joshua said to the Lord in the presence of Israel: Oh [sun], stand still over Gibeon, O moon, over the valley of [Aijalon]. So the sun stood still, and the moon stopped, till the nation avenged itself on its enemies, as it is written in the Book of Jashar. The sun stopped in the middle of the sky and delayed going down about a full day. There has never been a day like it before or since, a day when the Lord listened to a man. Surely the Lord was fighting for Israel.\"",), "Farid Responds, “Apostate Prophet FINALLY Responded to Me!! (ft. David Wood)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h7J-0jpf3KI", ((420, "7:00–8:30"),), id: 1335)
