#passage(("The message of all the prophets was the same. They, the prophets of God, said to their people: to attain salvation, believe in a man who come to die for you? None of them taught that. They told their people: follow the law, follow the law of God, repent when you commit sins. That's what they taught them, which is what prophet Muhammad taught. So if anybody is actually going against the teachings of the prophets, it's what you believe.", "The Tawra says that [a] true prophet is a prophet that confirms the messages of the previous prophets? That's the corrupted Tawra narrative, that I don't need to follow. I couldn't care less if it says you need to do jumping jacks to be a true prophet. A prophet can easily prove that he's a prophet in many ways, even within the Tawra that you talk about, by the way. I can give you examples of that in [Deuteronomy], for example, where if a prophet says something about the future and it comes to pass, then it is the word that the Lord has spoken. So if a prophet prophesizes the future and it comes to pass, that's evidence that this is a prophet coming from God. In Muhammad, I can give you tens of prophecies.", "A lot of Christians, they're coming and they think we need to justify what we believe in using their books. I'm going to say this in the clearest, loudest way: I couldn't care less what your book says. I couldn't care less what criteria your book gives. I couldn't care less if your book says Prophet Muhammad is this or Prophet Muhammad is that. What I care about is: objectively, rationally, is there evidence that Prophet Muhammad is a prophet of God? If I have undeniable, which I do, undeniable, convincing evidence that Prophet Muhammad is a messenger of God, why do I need to care about a book you have, which I've already demonstrated so many times how corrupted it is with the testimony of your own scholars? I don't need to care.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Educated Christian Challenges Muslim On Bible Corruption And Textual Criticism! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gYYmqGjrSmo", ((919, "15:19–15:58"), (965, "16:05–17:12"), (1043, "17:23–18:06"),), id: 1344)
