#passage(("Prophet Muhammad said, \"I'm sent by that creator, that God of the universe that created the universe.\" Now, if he wasn't truly a messenger of God, why would he be telling the people I'm a messenger of God? Why would he be doing that? For example, he did it for status?", "So Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him: we see his life. He was from the noble tribe of Quraysh in Mecca. When it comes to lineage, they were the most noble tribe; that's why they were the custodians of the [Kaaba]. He lived with his people before revelation 40 years. They had two titles for him: they called him the trustworthy, the honest, titles they gave him before prophethood. When they had disputes, they would go to him to solve their disputes.", "So when they were rebuilding the [Kaaba] and there was a last stone left, everyone wanted the [honour] of who puts the last stone. So they were fighting. So all the tribes came to him, because they know he was just, he was honest. He wouldn't take the side of those people over that people. So he gave them an idea. He said for the tribes to come, and then let's put this piece of clothing, and let's put the rock there, and everyone can carry the piece of clothing to the [Kaaba]. So everyone's participating. Then he took the rock and he put it in the corner. Now isn't this the ultimate level of status? He had all of this prior to messengerhood.", "There's a woman, richest woman in that time. She's called Khadijah, and all of the noble people wanted to marry her. But she went to Prophet Muhammad and married him because of his character. So he was married to the richest woman. He had money. He was running business and trading, buying and selling. And he was the most noble and most respected in that time. Nobody hated him. He wasn't an unknown person. No, he was the complete opposite of that.", "Then when he started the message, everyone started persecuting him. Now imagine: he had the status. But when he started calling people to worship God, in the beginning in Mecca, for nearly 13 years people wanted to kill him. He was persecuted. He would pray, people would come and put their foot and step on his neck, and they choke him to death. They want to kill him. They killed some of his companions already, like [Sumayyah] and other companions. They were put to death.", "One of the companions in the early Islam, [Bilal]: they used to bring a massive stone in the desert heat of Arabia, and they would put that stone on his belly, it's like burning. They put it in the middle of the desert, and they would turn him to disbelieve, and he would say, one god, one god. He wouldn't disbelieve, because this is the truth, this is what they believe in. The only reason the prophet had to leave Mecca, which is his own home that he said he loves, is because all of these tribes came together and they plotted to murder him. So he had to leave before they kill him. He had to migrate from Mecca to Medina.", "So now, for someone who wants status: all of them came to him and they said, we're going to make you a leader over us if you stop this, stop calling to the worship of other than our forefathers. You're going against our traditions. You're going against your own lineage. You're going against our ancestry of worshiping idols. Stop that and we'll make you our leader. Then the prophet refused: \"I'm sent by God.\" So would you say that this is wanting status or the opposite of wanting status?", "So many people don't know the life of the prophet Muhammad. When you look at how he lived his life, you realize how absurd it is that he would be doing for any other reason than truly being a messenger from God. So Prophet Muhammad, after he went to Medina, the people accepted him. They believed in his message, and later on he came back to Mecca as a conqueror, to take back his homeland with his people. And when he took those people that were persecuting him for 13 years, he let them go free. Imagine you tried to kill me for 13 years, but when I'm in position of power, I forgive you. Prophet said, I'm not going to blame you today, and he let them go.", "Now, before the [prophet's] death, all of Arabia was a Muslim empire under him. When someone would come to see the prophet, the prophet, peace be upon him, would be sitting on the floor with everyone else. So the person will come and say, where is Muhammad? Where is prophet Muhammad? Why? Because there's no king chair that is there, or gold and silver, and these fancy clothes. Do you need to ask who's the king if you enter upon the king? You don't really need to ask a question. When you enter upon the king, you recognize the king, because the king in most cases is sitting on a high throne with guards and all of the stuff. They will ask, where is prophet Muhammad? And then they will point at him.", "[Mu'adh], a companion, went to Sham, and he saw the kings of the Christians and the kings of the Jews being prostrated to. They used to prostrate to the kings, to people of high status, at that time. So [Mu'adh] said, I'm going to do that. So when he came and tried to do to the prophet, the prophet forbade them, said: you only prostrate to Allah, you don't prostrate to human beings. The prophet said to all of the Muslims: do not overpraise me like the Christians over praised Jesus and they made him a god. They elevated him from a human being to being a god. Do not overpraise me, but say the messenger of God and his servant. This is what you should call me.", "So when you look at the life of prophet Muhammad: with his wife Aisha, he would spend two entire month on dates and water. She reports one woman came to seek some charity. She knocked the doors; only one date in the house. She split the [date] and give her a half. This was the life that he was living. Is that someone who would live a life for status? Is that someone who would live a life seeking worldly stuff and seeking positioning?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Open-Minded Atheist Shifts His Entire Worldview After Encountering A Muslim! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7wdt9Q4kiMg", ((800, "13:20–13:57"), (839, "13:59–14:33"), (873, "14:33–15:19"), (919, "15:19–15:46"), (946, "15:46–16:16"), (976, "16:16–16:51"), (1011, "16:51–17:26"), (1055, "17:35–18:10"), (1090, "18:10–18:59"), (1139, "18:59–19:39"), (1181, "19:41–20:11"),), id: 1359)
