#passage(("Come with any alternative explanation. Remember this entire message, being persecuted for 13 years. He's coming telling people: worship God alone. I'm only a servant of God. Jesus was a messenger of God. Moses was a messenger of God. Abraham was a messenger of God. All coming to tell you about God, not about me. Do not worship any human beings. Do not pray to any human beings. Do not prostrate to any of the creation. Why would he be doing this?", "We fast Ramadan; the prophet fast Ramadan as well, but not only Ramadan: throughout the year, Monday and Thursday he would fast. He would pray the night prayer: it's the long prayer in the night, 8 to 20 units; can take you hours, that the prophet would pray in the night. Also, in the morning, not the five daily prayers, but after the five daily prayers, he would pray extra units of prayers, [Duha] prayer. His wife, she spoke about him, how he used to pray so much until his feet become in a bad condition, and then she came to him, she said, \"Oh prophet of Allah, why do you pray so long in the night when God has forgiven your sins?\" Then he told her, \"Should I not be a grateful servant? If God has forgiven me, I should be grateful. I should thank God for forgiving me.\" So he lives this life where he prays, he gives charity, he fasts, he helps people. And then he runs a community, and he's doing all of these things.", "The prophet was married to multiple wives. All of them saw him in his house. When you lie, when you're a hypocrite, when you're an evil person, you may act for 10, 20 minutes, but you're always exposed in secrecy. Now imagine not only lying to one wife; now imagine lying to multiple. You're either one day with the companions, one day with your wives. How long can you act? How long can you put a character for people? You're going to be exposed sooner or later. This was the life of the prophet.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Open-Minded Atheist Shifts His Entire Worldview After Encountering A Muslim! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7wdt9Q4kiMg", ((1797, "29:57–30:23"), (1839, "30:39–31:55"), (1917, "31:57–32:28"),), id: 1376)
