#passage(("Now the question is: how would a false prophet behave? And what would a false prophet teach the people?", "Prophet Muhammedﷺ was from the purest lineage in Mecca, the lineage of Quraish. They were the custodians of the Kaaba (House of God). They were the highest respected amongst their people. His grandfather was, you can say, the highest person amongst the Arabs, the most respected, the most honorable. So he was already having the status, the honor and the respect from the people.", "A man came to see him, and he was sitting down, and he said, \"Where is Muhammedﷺ?\" He asked this question because he was sitting down, wearing normal Bedouin clothing, on the floor, with the rest of the people. He didn't even know which one is your Prophet! They had to point at him! Cause he's not sitting on a chair as a king who's looking for authority, when you enter you know straightaway this is the king. He used to command them not to stand for him when he enters! The Prophetﷺ said, \"Do not overpraise me like the Jews and Christians overpraised Jesus,\" and they raised him to be God!", "An authoritarian, you can see his relationship. If he's got someone (who you can say is a servant), you can see in his relationship with his servant. As an authoritarian, he will abuse that servant. Prophet Muhammedﷺ had a servant, he was called Anas Ibn Malik. He said: \"I served the Prophet of Allah for 10 years. He never frowned in my face. He never told me about something I didn't do, why you didn't do it? or about something I did, why I did it?\" Not only that; but Anas رضي الله عنه (may Allah be satisfied with him), he said that whenever other people used to blame, the Prophetﷺ used to stop them and say, \"If Allah willed, it would have happened. Don't blame him!\" If you love Authority, it will be shown in your behavior.", "So why else would he do it for? Money, women, for example? First, he was married to one of the richest women in Mecca, Khadija bint Khuwaylid. He worked for her, trading, in the beginning. And other people were trying to marry her, but she refused them and she chose Prophet Muhammedﷺ, because he was known to be Trustworthy and Honest among the people. So, she is the one who proposed to him, if you put it in that way. So, Prophet Muhammedﷺ had all the money he needed. All the wives of prophet Muhammedﷺ were widows and [divorcees], except one. Many of them were older than him, like the first wife Khadijah. So if he wanted women, he would have had Harems: young virgins of Persia and Roma.", "So what other reasons would he be doing this? A lot of Christians, malicious Christians, would say this: is it possible that he was taking this information from Satan, trying to call people away from God? Satan would never command someone to worship God Alone. Would Satan command people to give charity? Would Satan command people to be good to their neighbors? Would Satan tell all the Arabs to stop worshipping Idols? He should be happy to worshipping idols other than God. That's why in the Bible you would see Jesus, when they called him Satan or someone sent by Satan, he said to them that Satan cannot go against himself! Meaning, he doesn't go against his teachings. So, Jesus is saying: a proof that I'm not sent by Satan is my teachings are not in correlation to what Satan likes and teaches.", "So if Prophet Muhammedﷺ could not have been any of these things, then what could he have been? And he's the only messenger that affirmed the previous Messengers. The only other religion that believes in Jesus other than Christianity is Islam. So if he is not a prophet of God, there has to be a logical explanation to what he is. Also, how would he bring the scripture that Arabs are not able to imitate? How would he prophesied about the future, and all of these things happen? How would he bring a Quran that has information that no one knew in the past, about the natural world and about history? How would that be possible if he's not a true prophet of God?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Lady Skeptical About The Prophet of Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QIxWrKc_J_Y", ((829, "13:49"), (862, "14:22–14:37"), (894, "14:54–15:24"), (939, "15:39–16:37"), (1030, "17:10–18:23"), (1130, "18:50–19:57"), (1205, "20:05–20:40"),), id: 1380)
