#passage(("Harris misunderstood what's going on here. The words [min al-fajr] were not added to explain what's going on in the verse. If the prophet, peace be upon him, wanted to explain a verse, he would simply do it; there's no need to add anything to the revelation for that purpose. Furthermore, most of the companions were aware of what was meant by the white thread, and the reason is because they're familiar with pre-Islamic poetry. So the correct question would be: why add the words to the verse? And this hadith explains the whole situation: \"I ate suhur with the messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, when it was daybreak, but the sun had not yet risen.\"", "In other words, the time of fasting early on in Islam was before sunrise, and after the inclusion in the verse, the time of the fasting change to al-fajr, which is the actual break of dawn. We also have [Tabi'in] like [Masruq] affirming that this was when fasting took place early on in Islam. Refer to al-Hazami for more on the subject of the abrogation of the timing of fasting.", "To reiterate, the addition of the words to the verse had nothing to do with explaining what was meant or explaining something that was left out, but it had to do with an actual change in the timing of when fasting was supposed to occur. Now, if the verse was an explanation, it would have added the words meaning sunrise instead of the break of dawn. How exactly is this a convenient revelation, when it means that fasting is longer by around an hour and a half? Why would anyone starve themselves and starve their community for an extra hour and a half of every day in Ramadan? In other words, this is an inconvenient revelation, in which the prophet, peace be upon him, had nothing to gain whatsoever.", "Now let's go through the actual hadith and let's see what Harris had left out. [Zayd ibn Thabit] is quoting Ibn Maktoum saying, \"O messenger of Allah, by Allah, if I were capable of jihad, then I would participate in jihad,\" and he was a blind man. So Allah revealed to his messenger, peace be upon him, while his thigh was against my thigh, and it became so heavy that I was worried it was about to fracture my thigh. Then he was relieved of it, meaning he was relieved of the revelation. So Allah had revealed to him, \"except those who are disabled.\"", "In other words, when the verse was revealed something supernatural occurred, and the revelation itself weighed down upon the prophet, peace be upon him, and he could actually feel it. This is of course similar to another hadith we find in Ahmed, number 24868, in which he reports that when the prophet, peace upon him, would receive the revelation while on a camel, the camel would be forced to sit down due to the weight of the revelation.",), "Farid Responds, “The Desperation of Harris Sultan”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_1i49UqIwqU", ((169, "2:49–3:44"), (227, "3:47–4:22"), (265, "4:25–5:21"), (385, "6:25–7:08"), (432, "7:12–7:41"),), id: 1386)

