#passage(("Some people, who are void of brains, would use the term \"delusion\". And delusion is that which you believe in something contrary to the compelling evidence. This compelling evidence, clear evidence: like the sun exists, but your delusion of saying the sun doesn't exist, contrary to the compelling evidence that you can see in front of you with your eyes, and you can feel its heat! So they would describe the Prophet Muhammadﷺ with that. But looking at the life of the Prophetﷺ, do you think over 100,000 people will follow someone who's got any sense of delusion? You think over 100,000 people (the Companions of the Prophetﷺ before his death; over 100,000 in the last lecture, that's a Khutbah (sermon) that he gave, خُطبة الوداع), you think they're not going to notice that?", "When the Prophet Muhammadﷺ, his son Ibrahim died, the people said that \"he is a Messenger of God\". Why is he a Messenger of God? Because his son died, there was an eclipse, solar eclipse: \"Ah! This is evidence!\" You get these people with the superstitious things. Now someone who is delusional, meaning believe in something completely contrary to the evidence, the first thing he's going to say: \"Of course, this is happening because of me!\" Because in this, it's a perfect situation, because the Prophet did not say it himself; they said it on his behalf. They are the ones who said that this happened because of the death of the son of the Prophet Muhammadﷺ.", "But the Prophet Muhammadﷺ, what did he say? He said, \"This is a sign of God; it does not happen for the life or death of anyone,\" \"and if you see it, rush to prayer\". \"Rush to prayer\", which shows that the Prophetﷺ knew what he was talking about. He was not just believing something randomly. He was looking at these events, getting Revelation, and delivering it to the people the way he received it from Allahﷻ.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Islam Unveiled: Compelling Evidence For The Truthfulness Of Islam | Chicago - USA | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AUFsBco_CF0", ((774, "12:54–13:38"), (821, "13:41–14:19"), (864, "14:24–14:48"),), id: 1387)
