#passage(("During the tenth year after the hijra, the prophet, peace be upon him, was visited by some priests from [Najran], and Hassan al-Basri reports that when this occurred there was a discussion, a debate, over the subject of the identity of the father of Jesus, peace be upon him. The prophet, peace be upon him, rebuked them for their views, and they simply asked him: well then, who is the father of Jesus? Al Hasan states that he waited until he received the following revelation: \"Indeed, the example of Jesus to Allah is like that of Adam. He created him from dust, then he said to him, Be, and he was.\" By that, of course, the verse is affirming that Jesus, like Adam, didn't have a father.", "The Quran then includes this order directed at the prophet, peace be upon him: \"Then whoever argues with you about it after this knowledge has come to you, say: Come, let us call our sons and your sons, our women and your women, ourselves and yourselves, then supplicate earnestly together and invoke the curse of Allah upon the liars among us.\" Upon this, the prophet came forward with his son-in-law and cousin Ali, his daughter Fatima, and his grandsons al-Hassan and Hussain. The priests from [Najran] did not see this through; instead they withdrew from the challenge to curse one another.", "But how is this proof of prophethood? Firstly, this shows the confidence of the prophet, peace be upon him, in that if they actually accepted this challenge, they would have definitely been cursed. The prophet, peace be upon him, was a believing man, and his sincerity was shown through his actions. Now, if he had his doubts about his claims, he wouldn't have brought his family along with him and risked having them getting cursed.", "Now keep in mind that this is a curse that's supposed to occur immediately. It's not one of those things like whoever dies first is cursed, or anything like that. He brought his family there to show that they would be cursed on the spot. This was something that was supposed to occur at that specific moment. Ibn Abbas mentions that if the priests actually accepted this challenge, they would have returned home to [Najran] finding their families dead and their wealth gone. And due to this, the priests returned and refused to accept this challenge.", "Now let's assume that the supernatural simply never occurred. What then? Well, if that was the case, then this would have looked bad upon the prophet, peace be upon him, because nothing would happen. You'd expect something to occur to those, or the family of those, that accept this challenge, and yet nothing occurring would simply cause people to doubt in the prophet, peace be upon him, and his claim of prophethood. So the question is, once again: why would you even put yourself in that situation? Why would you risk that, unless you were certain that they would actually be cursed? And that is why this is a proof of prophethood.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood 23: Challenge to Priests”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LWpc_FxVFLc", ((3, "0:03–0:52"), (52, "0:52–1:31"), (93, "1:33–2:12"), (132, "2:12–2:48"), (172, "2:52–3:34"),), id: 1397)
// U FR208:1-35 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("There is not a group of people out there in the world that admire and love Jesus more than Christians, and when the prophet, peace be upon him, dealt with them and interacted with them, he went completely against them by claiming that they were wrong about the identity of Jesus. Now, the claim that the prophet, peace be upon him, made, which is that Jesus is a prophet, a man, and not divine and not God, is a very risky claim, because he's putting his prophethood on the line. Isn't it likelier that the Christians are correct about the identity of Christ?", "So a false prophet would look at the different routes that they could take, and there are two routes that seem to be somewhat safe. Now, after accepting the claim that Christians made about Christ being God, a false prophet could either accept that Christ was indeed God, or that Christ lied about being God. Instead, the prophet, peace be upon him, rejected both and said that no, Christians are wrong about Christ; he never claimed to be God.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood 13: Identity of Jesus”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L3glkmDo4T8", ((11, "0:11–0:53"), (56, "0:56–1:30"),), id: 1398)
// U FR052:120-126 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("What you don't expect is what Muhammad, peace be upon him, did, because he didn't have access to all these texts and these lexicons. He didn't even have an Arabic Bible. However, despite that, he went to the Christian community, stared at them at the face, and said to them, \"You're incorrect about your ideas and your views of Jesus and your understanding of who God was.\" And he taught them their own religion while affirming that Jesus is a Messiah.",), "Farid Responds, “Did Jesus Claim to be God? [D. Wood Loses Debate]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6EMi_gjBrfk", ((650, "10:50–11:25"),), id: 1399)

=== 20.10 Knowledge of previous prophets and the Prophet's illiteracy
// NODE P04.C03.S10
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR209:1-42 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("One of the more interesting aspects of the Quran that's often overlooked is about the information that we find within it about previous prophets, prophets like Noah, Abraham, Moses, Aaron and Jesus. Now, the reason that this is a sign of prophethood is because the prophet, peace be upon him, couldn't read; he was illiterate. He never read the Bible, and he never learned this stuff from rabbis or monks. The prophet, peace be upon him, was raised with the Meccans; he lived with them, he traveled with them, he married from among them; he was a part of their community. This community did not observe him learning this information from Jews or Christians, and they did not know of this information themselves.", "\"This is from the news of the unseen which we reveal to you, O Muhammad. You knew it not, neither you nor your people, before this. So be patient; indeed, the best outcome is for the righteous.\" The verse affirms that the people should not know of the accounts mentioned about the story of Noah. \"This is from the news of the unseen which we reveal, O Muhammad, to you\": and this verse was revealed about the life of Joseph, or Yusuf, and this information was information that his people did not know of.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood 12: Previous Prophets”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mnkgbpebziM", ((5, "0:05–0:50"), (52, "0:52–1:22"),), id: 1400)
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML030:366-438 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("If you study the life of Prophet Muhammad, this is the argument I'm going to make: there isn't any other rational explanation that you can come up to other than he was a messenger of God. You as a Christian should appreciate this, I would say. Why? Because it's in line with what you believe in. You believe in what? That there is one God. You believe that idol worship is false. You believe that you should be good to our parents. You should be good to our neighbors. You should feed the poor. You should fast for the sake of God. You believe in all of these things. Prophet Muhammad in his community, if he wanted the worldly desires or power or any of that, he would go against all of these teachings. If he was not a true messenger of God, he wouldn't be in line with these teachings.", "Prophet Muhammad came: you know how the community was? Women were like cattle. They would bury daughters alive because they believed they're a shame. They would bury them as infanticide, little babies. Women would be walking the street. They would circulate the Kaaba naked, completely. Women would have no inheritance, no rights whatsoever; they're seen as a baggage in the society. And they would worship idols; they'd make idols from dates and pray to it.", "Now, Prophet Muhammad comes. Then he says to the people, after revelation comes to him, there's only one God. He says to them, \"Believe in Jesus. Believe in Moses. Believe in Abraham. Believe in Noah.\" He didn't just say believe in me. No. Jesus sent by God. Moses sent by God. Abraham sent by God. And then he comes and he says to them: be good to your neighbor. Prophet said he's not a believer, the one who sleeps and his neighbor is hungry. You sleeping and you know your neighbor is hungry, you're not even a believer. To that extent. Allah commanded you to worship him and be good to your parents. It's the second thing that God has commanded you in the Quran. Humble yourself. Humiliate yourself for them. Prophet said he's not from us who doesn't revere our older and respect our younger.", "And he comes and he says you have to pray to God. Prostrate. And in that prayer submit to God alone. Brought something called tawhid, monotheism: alone to the creator; nobody is associated with God. The biggest sin is shirk, associating with God someone else. So Prophet Muhammad came with that. Fast. Give charity, obligatory charity, one of the five pillars. So the question is, if he wasn't a true messenger of God, would he be doing any of this? No purpose, no gain from this.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Wise Christian Questions The Consistency Of The Teachings Of Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QEeureFKaFM", ((959, "15:59–16:35"), (995, "16:35–17:09"), (1029, "17:09–17:49"), (1073, "17:53–18:24"),), id: 1401)
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR209:43-70 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("These verses were revealed in Mecca, when he was surrounded and oppressed by his enemies. His tribe, Quraysh, were very well aware that he had no access to this information, that he was illiterate, and the Quran confirms this: \"And you did not recite before any scripture, nor did you inscribe one with your right hand; then otherwise the falsifiers would have had cause for doubt.\" Remember, the Quran is a text that's recited publicly. The prophet, peace be upon him, recited this to the Qurashis publicly. They heard these things from him, and if he said something that was untrue, he would have been called out for it. If he read \"and you did not recite before it any scripture\", and in reality he did, he would have been exposed for uttering these words. So once again, he had information that he was not supposed to have.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood 12: Previous Prophets”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mnkgbpebziM", ((83, "1:23–2:20"),), id: 1402)
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML236:1233-1255 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("Prophetﷺ, he did not read, he didn't write, and this information was written by his direct Companions, stored, recorded, and we have them today, directly from his Companions, directly from the people around him. And Allahﷻ, one of His wisdoms, is to show us: how is he able to bring this Quran? Let alone everything else that we said. How was he able to produce this Quran? And these criminals, evildoers, they'll say: maybe he did this, maybe he was eloquent. So Allahﷻ left no chance, no opportunity, no door open for them, except to accept that he's a Messenger of Allah.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Islam Unveiled: Compelling Evidence For The Truthfulness Of Islam | Chicago - USA | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AUFsBco_CF0", ((4053, "1:07:33–1:08:27"),), id: 1403)
