=== 20.11 Aisha as a witness to prophethood
// NODE P04.C03.S11
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR250:1-43 | Farid Responds | 4
#passage(("This is a different way of looking at proofs for prophethood. Usually it's done by focusing on matters like miracles, matters like prophecies, matters like what you find in previous scriptures, the eloquence of the Quran, many different methods when one looks into the proofs of the prophet. However, in this particular subject I wanted to focus on the question of why did the Sahabah themselves believe.", "Do you know how Aisha came to Islam? She was born into Islam. However, does that really change anything? She knew him arguably better than anyone else. She outlived the prophet, peace be upon him, by almost 50 years, and in that time she was seen as arguably the greatest scholar that we have in Islam. She taught more than most. She has more hadiths than most. She made a huge change.", "Some of these topics are going to seem like: why are you even going into this? This lecture is focusing on not just the Muslims but those who are extremely skeptical. So the first subtopic will be: did she claim to believe in Islam? The one after that will be: was she sincere? Was she truthful? And then we're going to get into the topic of what are the proofs that not necessarily made her a Muslim but kept her a Muslim: why was she a sincere believer in Islam?", "This is the simplest point really. Every single hadith that you read that [Aisha] is narrating — and these reports come through several companions, a large number — you can find them in pretty much every book of hadith: [Bukhari], Muslim, the six books, any book that you can think of, you're gonna find narrations of Aisha. It's undeniable. What do we get from that? We get that she existed. And we get that she believed, in this simplest way of putting it. We're going to go a bit deeper into that. But at least this establishes her outer belief in the prophet, peace be upon him.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood - A'isha”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R2n1dP6j9iI", ((13, "0:13–0:55"), (59, "0:59–2:06"), (134, "2:14–2:58"), (183, "3:03–4:23"),), id: 1404)
// U FR250:63-348 | Farid Responds | 24
#passage(("So was Aisha sincere in her beliefs inwardly, not just outwardly? We have very explicit narrations that do imply that she did believe in Islam, that she did believe in this message. So for example, it's narrated that she used to fast every single day. Why would she put herself through such a thing if she didn't believe in Islam? She also lived a very [ascetic] life, and she gave away 70,000 dirhams to charity while living in such a poor state that she used to stitch up her own clothing. When we have our clothes torn up, we just toss them out, buy some new clothes. Everything I'm quoting is authentic according to Sunni standards of hadith, Muslim standards of hadith.", "Now, some people might feel like these reports, they're very explicit. They're so explicit that I'm having doubts. However, there are implicit reports. And sometimes the implicit reports, you feel like there's no reason for anyone to make this up. There's no reason for me to doubt this. They're not that direct. Some of them, they're just so tame. I don't want to say mundane, but they really have nothing to do with her faith.", "So for example, Aisha teaches women that after your period of menstruation has finished you don't need to make up the prayers that you missed out on. Just a random hadith. Why would she teach women that? Why is she going out of her way to educate women about this? It's because she believed. [Abdurrahman ibn Abi] Bakr, her brother: \"Woe onto the heels from hellfire.\" And this, of course, has to do with doing the ablution in a correct manner. She's reminding him or teaching him about this. Why would she teach him this? Because she believed in it.", "She tells women that it's forbidden to mourn when someone has passed away for more than three days, with the exception of it being your husband. Why would Aisha tell women stop mourning after three days? It's because she believed in it. She taught the Muslims that it's forbidden to kill certain types of snakes. Why would she do that? Aren't all snakes dangerous? It's because she believed in this. This is [Bukhari]. She taught people that the animals can hear the dead being punished in the grave ([Bukhari] and [Sahih] Muslim).", "[When the Prophet] was well, he used to recite the last two chapters of the Quran onto his hands and he used to wipe himself as a form of fortification, a means to cure, because of course the words of Allah are a form of a cure. And [when he] was very sick, having trouble struggling to recite, [Aisha] would be the person reciting onto his hand and wiping him with his own hand. Why would she do this? Because she believed. If you think about it, it's not farfetched to believe that the wife of a prophet believed in him. I'm really going out of my way to hammer this message across to those that see [Aisha] as someone who didn't believe in his message.", "She believed that his actions were precursors to revelation. So for example, on the third night he didn't [pray], saying, \"I fear that it might be made obligatory upon the Muslims if I continue to pray.\" This is Muslim. And the description of the prayer: why would Aisha go into the very details of his prayer? It's because she believed.", "[Aisha] narrates that this occurs outside of Medina. They were coming back, I believe, from one of the battles — I believe this was in the sixth year, if I'm not mistaken — and basically [Aisha] is left behind. When they enter Medina, rumors erupt that there was something going on, and that there was [zina] that occurred. This is Aisha's narration, by the way. Narration can be found inside Muslim. And [Aisha] says that she was upset that a lot of people doubted her. And she was hoping that a verse would be revealed to clear her from this charge. And after a month of this happening, verses were revealed indeed clearing her off this charge.", "The mother of Aisha said to Aisha, \"Go to him.\" And she said, \"I will not go to him. [Allah] cleared me from this. I only thank Allah.\" What does this say? That Aisha believed. And what's interesting is, if Aisha didn't believe, then she would have been like: why isn't he just saying that nothing happened? Why is he waiting a month? And of course this in itself is proof: one wouldn't leave his wife under suspicion for a month.", "Another example: in the ninth year after the [hijrah] — of course there are verses in the Quran that speak about this — he gave the wives the option to stay with him or leave him. And the verse says, \"If you should desire the worldly life and its [adornments], then come, I will provide you and give you a gracious release.\" Verse number 28. Now this might seem a bit strange: why would they receive the worldly life if they left him? Well, first of all, it's forbidden for any one of his wives to marry another man after this, if they accepted, if they said, \"We're going to stay your wife.\" This is one year before he passes away, a year and a few months. If he passes away, they're stuck. They cannot get married to another man. And secondly, it's because they lived really tough lives. They only ate water and dates, as Aisha narrates herself. And Aisha chose, implying that she believed. And due to this she spent the next 50 years single.", "So was Aisha truthful? There are many reasons to believe that Aisha is truthful, including all that I've just mentioned: her belief, the reasons for her faith indicates her truthfulness. But moreover, we have explicit statements by companions, and those that came after, that she was truthful. The simplest reason to believe that she was truthful is the Sahabah took knowledge from her. Abu [Musa] narrates that the greater scholars among the Sahabah took knowledge from her. Aisha had more knowledge than all the wives of the prophet, peace be upon him. Naturally you will not take knowledge from someone who you deemed to be a liar.", "She corrects the companions in their understanding of narrations and their statements. If Aisha was seen as someone who was unreliable, then they wouldn't let her do that: \"Who are you to correct me? I know this.\" [But Aisha's] word was often final.", "Now, there's something to keep in mind: we shouldn't be skeptical to that degree if there's no proof in the first place that she was a doubtful person, if she was a liar. If you don't have proof of that, there's no reason for us to assume that every single person is a liar. But for the purpose of this lecture, I'm going to be using implicit evidence. I'm not going to be appealing to supernatural reports. I'm going to be appealing to believable reports. In most cases, these reports are corroborated by other companions.", "Aisha narrated that — now, we're not talking about the mandatory prayer, we're talking about the non-mandatory, the voluntary — he used to pray four prostrations before, two after, two after, nine prostrations, the night prayers, and two before. Why would he do that if he didn't believe in what he was doing? This is Muslim. When he grew in age, he didn't stop praying the volunteer prayers. He decreased from nine night prostrations to seven (Muslim). He would even pray when he was sick ([Musnad] Ahmed).", "The length of his physical prostration, when he would bow down, would be the length of how one used to recite 50 verses from the Quran. That's how lengthy his prayer was: 18 prostrations, 10 minutes per prostration, so it's about half the night. And of course there is a verse in the Quran in which [Allah] instructs the prophet, makes it obligatory upon him to pray half the night. For what reason? He's not showing off, by the way. He's not doing this publicly; he's doing this privately. [Aisha] narrates that in one specific incident, \"I go towards the room and I find him praying inside.\" There were cracks on his feet due to the amount of time he used to spend in prayer, the amount of time he used to spend standing (Muslim). And what I find interesting is he used to wake up to pray with him as well. Why would he be annoying her? Why would he disturb her unless he really believed in this?", "Apart from his prayers, there's just so much: the [asceticism] I mentioned previously. She narrates they didn't have wheat bread for three days in a row. It's a rare occurrence for them to have an abundance of wheat bread (Muslim). In a specific report, once Aisha only had a single date, and the woman came; she had her daughters with her. They knocked and Aisha opened up; they needed the food. Aisha took the date and she gave it to the woman. She split it among her two daughters. They lived that life. They were really poor people. They didn't have anything.", "He used to wear heavy wool clothing (Muslim). He used to stitch his clothes up. He used to fix his sandals himself. So we often hear he used to take the fifth of the war booty. Is it for himself? If it's for himself, then why is he stitching up his clothes? Why is he just eating dates and drinking water? ([Bukhari].) He used to pay the debts with that money: the debts of those that passed away and weren't able to pay their own debts. He took it upon himself: \"I will pay your debts.\" Who does that? He died without leaving behind a dinar or a dirham or a sheep or a camel or a slave ([Musnad] Ahmed).", "Before passing away he called and said, \"Bring the money that we have left.\" They had seven or nine dinars. And when she brought the money, instead of saying, \"Keep it for you,\" he said, \"Give it away to charity.\" And he said something along the lines of, \"What shall become of Muhammad if he were to face Allah and this remained with him?\" (Ahmed).", "There are some narrations that prevent you from having an extreme veneration. For example: \"May Allah curse the Christians and the Jews, for they took the graves of their prophets as places of worship\" ([Bukhari], Muslim). Why? He doesn't want the Muslims to take his grave as a place of worship. Why would it matter to him if he didn't believe in what he was doing, if he didn't believe in monotheism that he was teaching?", "There's a verse in [an-Najm] that's somewhat ambiguous, that speaks about seeing him. Some assume that this had to do with him seeing Allah. And when Aisha was asked about this, she said, \"I know the answer to this. I asked him and he said, 'I did not see Allah. I saw [Jibreel].'\" Again, if he was making this up, he would have said, \"Yeah, I saw Allah.\" He is not allowing anyone to give him a higher status than he deserves ([Sahih] Muslim).", "And now finally, after all that, we can talk about why Aisha believed. We spoke about: did she claim to believe? Was she sincere? Was she truthful? Did she believe that he believed? And finally: why did she believe? Before getting into that, I just want to point out that Aisha is a very inquisitive person. She was critical. She was a thinker. She didn't just accept anything.", "So there's a report in which he says that those who are accounted for will be punished. \"But doesn't Allah say that there will be people that will have an easy judgment?\" He explains to her: this is for those that have their deeds shown to them. Those that are accounted for their deeds, those are the people that will be punished. So this shows that [Aisha] is not just going with the flow: \"Did he say the moon split? Okay, I'm going to believe that.\" And by the way, just a brief point: the Sahaba narrated that. People around the prophet, peace be upon him, narrated that. He didn't say it and everyone believed it.", "There are other reports that [give] a reason to have doubts. A very famous report, the agriculture hadith: he says, \"Why are you pollinating the plants?\" And they say, \"Well, it helps with the crops.\" And he says, \"It would be best if you did not pollinate the plants.\" And the crops died, and they said, \"[Ya Rasulullah], the crops died.\" And he said, \"Well, you know your worldly affairs better than I do.\" If it was one of these critics of Islam: \"A false prophet!\" A very interesting report, and this is Muslim.", "A prophecy before he passes away: his wives go to him and they ask him, \"Who's going to pass away first from among us?\" And he says — the wording in Arabic is quite nice — \"The fastest that catches up to me is she who has the longest hands.\" And what did the wives do? They started measuring their hands. Now, why would they do this? Because they believed. And who had the longest hands? It was [Sawda]. This was, I think, towards the end of the 10th year. Nine years after the prophet passed away, [Zaynab] was the first to pass away, not [Sawda].", "How did the wives react? What did Aisha say? She said, \"Then we understood that this was not about the physical length. This was a metaphor for she who gives to charity.\" Why did Aisha think in that manner? Why didn't she say, \"Oh, wow. False prophecy\"? It's because she's seen so much. She's been through so much. She knows for a fact that he has to be a prophet from God. There's no other explanation: \"I must have misunderstood this. It's not physical. It's not literal.\"",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood - A'isha”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R2n1dP6j9iI", ((391, "6:31–7:45"), (493, "8:13–8:59"), (539, "8:59–9:49"), (591, "9:51–10:54"), (657, "10:57–12:06"), (731, "12:11–13:28"), (820, "13:40–15:07"), (909, "15:09–15:44"), (950, "15:50–17:19"), (1047, "17:27–18:46"), (1130, "18:50–19:20"), (1168, "19:28–20:20"), (1237, "20:37–21:52"), (1314, "21:54–23:25"), (1411, "23:31–24:39"), (1483, "24:43–25:53"), (1555, "25:55–26:33"), (1595, "26:35–27:25"), (1649, "27:29–28:11"), (1696, "28:16–28:55"), (1738, "28:58–30:26"), (1832, "30:32–31:19"), (1881, "31:21–32:17"), (1943, "32:23–33:02"),), id: 1405)
// U FR250:482-499 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("These are just some of the matters that have reached us from Aisha's reasons. We don't know what else Aisha saw. And surely she saw a lot of things. Keep in mind that [Aisha] is just one part of the puzzle. Imagine we had this talk with Ali and Omar and all the major companions. The picture would get clearer and clearer as to why they believed. This is one specific person out of 100,000. The Medani community was like 10,000. That's a lot of people that saw a lot of things and had their own reasons.", "Those that want to get deeper into this: you'll find a lot of things in the Quran, especially if you study reasons of revelation; you will find evidence for Islam that you did not see before. You will find matters that sometimes will be overlooked by scholars. Read a hadith with new eyes.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood - A'isha”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R2n1dP6j9iI", ((2858, "47:38–48:35"), (2923, "48:43–49:09"),), id: 1406)

=== 20.12 The transformation of the Companions
// NODE P04.C03.S12
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR075:14-45 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("What I did in this work is I went through four examples. I wasn't looking at specific individuals and their conversions. What I sought out to do is to look at the community: why did the community as a whole, why did these major tribes convert to Islam? There are many books that have been written in the past that are simple direct proofs of prophethood — even we have modern works — and what they would usually do is they just simply provide you with examples of miracles performed by the prophet, peace be upon him, and things of that nature. What I wanted to do in this one was: I didn't focus on these specific actions as much as I focused on the community, these people that were once upon a time polytheists. What caused them to abandon polytheism and accept monotheism? What caused them to accept an afterlife? Because the pagan Arabs didn't believe in afterlife.",), "Farid Responds, “My New Book!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NtfqY5BFGCg", ((51, "0:51–2:22"),), id: 1407)
// U FR016:1-89 | Farid Responds | 7
#passage(("I came across this really cool report. It's something that I would actually use as an indirect proof of prophethood. And this would fall under two categories. One is the sincerity of the prophet, peace be upon him. And two is how he changed people. And the second is something that's not really utilized much.", "The common example that at least I usually bring up is Abu Dhar al-Ghifari. The tribe of Ghifar, they're known for being [raiders]. They were situated in the north of Mecca, somewhere around there. And what they would do is they would raid the tribes that would actually go to Hajj. Imagine that: you're going to Hajj, and then out of nowhere Ghifar pounces on you and they raid your goods. So, they convert to Islam, they change, they stop raiding. Abu Dhar himself becomes someone that not only is against the accumulation of wealth, but condemns those that accumulate wealth. So, what a change, 180°.", "Another example is the story of Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, or at least a part of it. Here's the narration. It's in Sahih Muslim. Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman was not at the Battle of Badr. And he says here, \"I was not at the Battle of Badr. The only thing that prevented me was I was with my father al-Husayl.\" This was my first time realizing that his father is not named Yaman. And he says the Kuffar, they seized them. And this is close to Badr. And we know that through a narration that you find in Mustadrak al-Hakim, which provides additional details. So he says that Quraysh said to him, \"You want to Muhammad?\" — as in, you want to go to Muhammad. And they responded, \"No, we don't. We just want to go to Medina.\" So they took oaths from Hudhayfah and from his father that they would go to Medina and they would not support the Prophet, peace be upon him, in his battle.", "This is why this is so interesting: Hudhayfah bin al-Yaman al-Absi. He's from Abs. Banu Abs is the fiercest, in my opinion, most dangerous Bedouin tribe. They're all about going to war with everyone. In any case, he is not among Abs. He's living in Medina. What's going on there? Why isn't Hudhayfah and his father with their tribe? I don't know too many companions from the tribe of Abs.", "So, I looked into his biography, and his father killed someone from his own tribe and then escaped to Medina, Yathrib at the time. And Medina is actually south of where Abs lived. So, he left his tribe and sought the protection of the people of Medina, specifically of the clan of Banu Abd al-Ashhal, which was led by Sa'd ibn Mu'adh. And he married into them. And not only that: because of his new-found allegiance to them, people started to refer to him as al-Yamani. His name is al-[Husayl]. They called him al-Yaman because he is someone who allied with the people of al-Yaman. And the Aws and al-Khazraj are from the people of al-Yaman.", "So, Hudhayfah says that they went to the prophet, peace be upon him, directly and they told him of what just happened. And he said to them, \"Leave\" — as in, go back to Medina. \"We stick with our oaths, and we seek the aid of Allah against them.\"", "So, there's a couple of things here. One is the change. Who was al-Yaman? Al-Yaman is someone that betrayed his tribe, killed someone from his tribe, and had to escape, and allied with another group of people. Look at the change in Yaman. Instead of someone that breaks his ties, now he's sticking with his oaths that he gave to his enemies. What a change.",), "Farid Responds, “Two Indirect Proofs of Prophethood from One Hadith”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NWVeQBf8snY", ((0, "0:00–0:25"), (25, "0:25–1:11"), (71, "1:11–2:38"), (163, "2:43–3:48"), (233, "3:53–4:50"), (298, "4:58–5:28"), (334, "5:34–6:06"),), id: 1408)

