#passage(("I recently came across this discussion between Hamza and Avery about whether there will be any prophets coming after Jesus, peace be upon him, or not. And of course it's a significant question, because, Christians, if there are going to be prophets after Jesus, then naturally you're going to have to take their claims of prophethood seriously instead of just dismissing everyone.", "[Avery] initially accepts this matter literally, and he affirms that yes, this is speaking about literal prophets, but denying that there will be any prophets that will come after Jesus. Later in the discussion he realizes that he's in a pickle, and that Hamza is right, and therefore he shifts his answer and he says no, what's meant here by prophets is that these are people that're just proclaiming the message. And this is absolutely ridiculous, especially if you examine the context, because when speaking about false prophets, Matthew in chapter 7 quotes Jesus saying, \"On that day many will say to me, 'Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name, and cast out demons in your name, and do many mighty works in your name?'\" So in these verses these are not people who are just simply proclaiming the message, but these are people that are coming with great signs, and that's why Jesus wants you to test those people.", "Now, that being said, I understand why [Avery] is being so unreasonable in his position and vehemently denying that this is what Jesus is saying, and that's because of his reaction towards Islam. At the end of the day, if Jesus is saying that you need to actually sit down and test these prophets, then that would provide the prophethood of Muhammad, peace be upon him, some legitimacy, because Muhammad, peace be upon him, is someone that did a great job in spreading monotheism and giving legitimacy to the previous prophets, and causing the peninsula that rejected the existence of an afterlife to ultimately believe in the existence of an afterlife — seems like a credible candidate for a future [prophet].", "So how does Avery justify his view while using the Bible? There are a bunch of verses that he quotes in order to prove that there are no prophets after Jesus, peace be upon him. Well, Hebrews isn't quoting Jesus. Avery, you are choosing the words of an anonymous author that clearly has never met Jesus over the words of Jesus himself in the gospel of Matthew. \"These last days\" doesn't need to be from the time of Jesus until the day of judgment. It could simply be referring to the last few years or last few decades; it's referring to the time of Jesus himself, those last days from the time in which those words were written. So the brother was right: you were going to quote some verses that were not explicit at all. And again, Jesus did not say those words.", "In Luke [16:16], Jesus says, \"The law and the prophets were until John; since then the good news of the kingdom of God, the gospel, is preached, and everyone forces his way into it.\" So according to Jesus the law and the prophets stopped at John the Baptist? Oh, they did? Luke [16:17]: \"But it is easier for heaven and earth to pass away than for one dot of the law to become void.\" Jesus isn't claiming that the law has ended, but he was just speaking about how people sought [guidance] before his time, before he brought the good news. So no: just like the law is no longer void, similarly there's no reason to assume that prophets have stopped coming, especially due to Matthew 7, that tells you to test future prophets.", "Now, with that in mind, I would like to remind the Christian audience to take the orders of Jesus seriously, and to actually keep an open mind in regards to future prophets, especially if it's a prophet who was extremely successful at spreading monotheism — I dare say more successful in spreading monotheism than Jesus himself.",), "Farid Responds, “Godlogic vs Hamza - Prophets after Jesus”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vFTPwJtkClU", ((0, "0:00–0:57"), (113, "1:53–3:02"), (182, "3:02–3:56"), (238, "3:58–6:40"), (400, "6:40–7:49"), (469, "7:49–8:17"),), id: 1425)

