#passage(("Here's the first problem. \"He's going to bring justice to the Gentiles\", because \"nations\" can include that he's going to the nations of the Jewish people. But why do translations not use the word Gentiles, while the [word] used is specifically in Hebrew about Gentiles specifically? Because they want to fit that some Christians say this is about Jesus. So if they put Gentiles, then they know they have an issue.", "This is the second games the translators play, so they can change what the verse is actually saying. The verse is not saying \"for his teaching\". Teaching is a very general word that can mean anything. It says the coastlands will wait for his Tawra. This guy's bringing a new Torah. It's not just like a normal person. He's bringing a new Tawra in Hebrew. So forget now about Moses's Torah. There's a new Tawra, a new divine concrete law that is coming. People in the coastlands are waiting for that new divine concrete law to come.", "The verse is saying specifically that this prophet, God will use him as a covenant to the Gentiles, as a light to the Gentiles. So this prophet is going to have a covenant with the Gentiles. He's going to bring a new Torah to the Gentiles. By the way, we are the Gentiles, you and me.", "Here God is referring specifically to idol worshippers. He's saying he's not going to give his glory to idols, specifically in context of Gentiles who are waiting for a man that is going to come to them as a covenant with a new law. And what is the covenant against? It's against this idol [worshipping] that is taking place. God is not going to give that glory. He's not going to allow this glory to be continued to be given to graven images and idols. God is saying, \"I'm not sharing it with these idols and people worshiping idols. I don't share my glory with anything.\"", "Allah says in the Quran, the covenant of Allah specifically has been given to us. Allah talks about Prophet Muhammad and how the covenant has been taken to him and how he's going to be a witness over us. All of these things are in the Quran.", "Silah is specifically a mountain in Saudi Arabia, Medina. Anyone can Google it, by the way: Mount Silah in Medina. Kedar is, by agreement with all scholars, the Arabs. Who is going to rejoice? The Arabs and the children of [Kedar], specifically Medina, Saudi Arabia: they're going to rejoice with a man that is going to come with a new law and a covenant. And the glory that has been given to the graven images: it only ended with prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him. Idol [worship] started in the deserts of Arabia before prophet Muhammad, after Abraham and after Ishmael, and it ended with the prophet Muhammad. He's the one who ended this glory that God is talking about here, that has been given to graven images.", "So there is here triumphing: a man of war triumphing over enemies. This is you misunderstanding biblical language. Nobody comes and says that God himself is going to come on earth and going to start walking and doing that. Does the Bible say the Lord will come down from Mount [Paran]? The Bible in Deuteronomy 33 specifically says about Moses: the Lord will come down from Mount [Paran]. It uses the same terminology about Moses. So the Lord is a representation of the Lord, and that can be a prophet and that can be a messenger. That can be an angel: who is wrestling Jacob in the Old Testament is \"the Lord\" wrestling Jacob. Who did God say to, \"I'm going to make you God to Pharaoh\"? You're not understanding biblical terminology, and that's the problem. We understand biblical terminology and Hebrew terminology, which is the sister language of Arabic. And we look at what the rabbis say; this is their own language. This is biblical language: to say that someone is a representative of the Lord, to call him the Lord.", "Capital and small, it's not there in the Hebrew. The reason they put it like that is what I was telling you before, which is: Christians, lay Christians, they start thinking, \"Oh, this is God, this is not God.\" When they like, they put a small G. When they don't like, they put a big G. But if you go to the text, it doesn't matter, because there is no small and capital letters in the text. For example, if you go to Psalms 82:6, it literally says Elohim, which is the reference to God, the word specifically God himself. The same Elohim is mentioned in Genesis 1:1. Genesis 1:1 talks about God creating the heavens and the earth: \"In the beginning, God created the heavens and the earth.\" So if you go to the translation in [82:6], it puts a small G. Why do you put a small G? They use it when they like, but in Genesis 1, they put a big G. So this big and small game played in the translations, I couldn't care less about it. In the text, when it talks about Mount Paron, when it talks about making Moses God, when it talks about the judges of the Jews, all of those representatives, they're all called directly God. The same word that is used for God himself is used for them. And this is biblical language.", "Yahweh, whichever way you say, we don't know how to say it, because it doesn't have vowels anyways. Nobody knows how to pronounce the word. But you're saying: if Yahweh is used, then it must be God.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Overconfident Christian Meets The Wrong Muslim! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EFSTD9c3Lec", ((2436, "40:36–40:58"), (2525, "42:05–42:38"), (2630, "43:50–44:10"), (2684, "44:44–45:17"), (2753, "45:53–46:05"), (2849, "47:29–48:34"), (2947, "49:07–51:06"), (3094, "51:34–53:12"), (3280, "54:40–55:02"),), id: 1427)
