#passage(("The claim that because the Bible does not mention [Ibrahim] building the [Kaaba], it is false: this is an argument from silence fallacy. Even if the Bible does not mention the [Kaaba] or [Ibrahim] building it, that is not evidence [Ibrahim] did not build it. The criteria of the Muslim is the Quran, not the Bible.", "The Bible does in fact mention Mecca and the [Kaaba]. Firstly, the Bible says [Ibrahim], when he was traveling around, he built many altars or places of worship. Now someone could make the argument that this is vague. So let's bring a verse which goes into more detail. In Psalms a place is mentioned which a people come for pilgrimage. People come to that place for Hajj. And the verse says Becca is a valley, and similarly Mecca is also a valley. B: it is called the house of Allah. It is called the house of God. C: it is called [Becca], which is the ancient name of Mecca, and it is the same word Allah uses in the Quran for Mecca. And D: it mentions that one day at the house of God is better than a thousand years anywhere else, which is reflected in the hadith, that anyone who prays in the haram at the [Kaaba] in Mecca will get 100,000 times more reward than anyone praying anywhere else. So for these reasons the [Kaaba] is mentioned in the Bible.", "But now let's respond to [Ridvan's] argument why Becca cannot be Mecca. [Ridvan] makes three false points here. Firstly, the entire chapter does not state the location of the valley of Becca. Nor does it say the valley of Becca is in Jerusalem. Secondly, the Hebrew says the pilgrims pass through the valley of Becca and then walk or travel to Zion, meaning Jerusalem. Psalms does not claim that the valley of Becca is located in Zion. Nothing in a chapter [rebuts] this point. No time limit between the two locations is mentioned. Nor is a distance between the valley of Becca and Zion given either. So the claim that Becca is Mecca cannot be refuted by simply showing the distance between Mecca and Jerusalem.", "Thirdly: another place in the Bible where the phrase Becca is used, to prove Becca is in Jerusalem. This is a [fallacious] argument. Firstly, the context in Psalms and Samuel are completely different. In Samuel, God tells David to attack his enemies in front of balsam trees. And yes, the word baka is mentioned here. That is because in Hebrew, Becca can also mean balsom. Moreover, a completely different valley is mentioned, the valley of Refaim. So these balsam trees mentioned in Samuel cannot be referring to the valley of [Becca], because they are in a completely different valley. The location of the battle is made clear in the surrounding verses: David is fighting the Philistines, and his battle is being fought in Palestine. And thirdly: the Muslims are only making this connection between Becca and Mecca because the words sound the same, or because a similar word is being used? Well, this is false. We are comparing the description of the valley of Becca and Mecca itself. And the description of the valley of Becca suits Mecca and the [Kaaba] like a glove. While Samuel makes no mention of pilgrimage, the house of God or the Valley of Becca, the context is clearly fighting and the location is clearly given. So this is a weak point.",), "Farid Responds, “[DEBUNKED] The Truth about the Kaaba - Apostate Prophet”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EYzTV_Q-nh0", ((412, "6:52–7:10"), (434, "7:14–8:32"), (512, "8:32–10:01"), (601, "10:01–11:20"),), id: 1441)

