#passage(("Now the only way for these Western academics to deal with these classical reports is by assuming that anything that goes against their narrative to be some sort of Islamic fabrication, and has to be dismissed. And this is somewhat common in academia. If you find a prophetic report that conflicts with your conclusion, just dismiss it: \"It's a fabrication. We shouldn't trust hadith.\"", "Now the hadith from the prophet, peace be upon him, in which he says \"I don't know if Dhul-Qarnain is a prophet or not\" – it's a wonderful hadith, because it even actually fits in the criterion of embarrassment. Think of it this way. The prophet, peace be upon him, is tested with knowledge of a historical figure, and instead of saying \"Yes, this is the Nabiyullah Alexander, alayhis salam\", and then he recounts the story which you find in the Quran, instead of that he says \"I don't know if he was a prophet or not\". What's the motivation for fabricating a report like that in the first place?", "They don't even bother to give you a reason as to why it's fabrication, and here are a few examples of how extreme they are when it comes to dismissing basic Islamic information. We're dealing with someone that believes that the Quran has multiple authors, that it wasn't necessarily written in the Hijaz, or at least those sections about Dhu al-Qarnayn weren't written in the Hijaz. And Professor Tomasso isn't even sure of the year of the Hijra. He says we don't know. Now, if you dismiss everything that we know about Islam historically, I can completely understand how your theory could hold some weight. But if you examine any of the historical evidence that is tied to Islam, naturally you're going to reject the Quran was borrowing from the Syriac Alexander Legend.",), "Farid Responds, “Dhu al-Qarnayn ≠ Alexander [ft. Prof. Tommaso Tesei]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tKyy0weOjow", ((474, "7:54–8:21"), (501, "8:21–8:58"), (561, "9:21–12:09"),), id: 1455)

