#passage(("\"The camels will be abandoned\": the prophet specifically mentions young female camels, instead of generally camels. He says young female camels. You have some modern Scholars that have understood this to mean people will stop riding young female camels. Classical Scholars did not get that, because they're like, why did the prophet say young female? Well, that's naturally because they're so expensive. There's no reason for us to assume that this is referring to all camels. To refer to all camels, he would use [jamal], for example.",), "Farid Responds, “Destroying Qadianis with ONE Question”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=leu0Je7sQA8", ((176, "2:56–4:04"),), id: 1490)

=== 23.6 The prophecies of Mirza Ghulam Ahmad
// NODE P04.C06.S06
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR133:1-37 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("Some of you have been Ahmedis for 20 years, 30 years, 40 years. Wouldn't now be the time for you to test yourself and test why you believe in Ahmadiya? If you've been upon falsehood your whole life, wouldn't now be the best time for you to turn it around and to follow the truth? You owe it to yourself to test yourself. I want to bring the truth to you, so please keep an open mind.",), "Farid Responds, “Failed Prophecies of Mirza Ghulam Ahmad”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gb9bWdWNblY", ((24, "0:24–1:21"),), id: 1491)
// U FR132:1-175 | Farid Responds | 8
#passage(("It is the 11th of December 2022, and Morocco has just defeated Portugal at the quarterfinals of the World Cup. Imagine I said that I received a revelation that said that Morocco is going to win the World Cup this year. That is the prophecy. Unfortunately, Morocco lose to France and they don't even make it to the finals, and people start coming up to me and saying, \"Fareed, the prophecy had failed\", and I respond by saying, \"Wait, even though they did not win the tournament, what they did win was the hearts of the people, so the prophecy did come to pass.\"", "Now this is the sort of thing that you actually find: the claim that the revelations that he received from Allah are a hundred percent correct, however he would at times misinterpret the meaning of the Revelation, and based on that, of course, he would make prophecies that would naturally fail. What Ahmadis need to be aware of is that the prophecy is the [yardstick] that determines whether someone is a prophet or not. So if you keep on doing things like reinterpreting prophecies when they go wrong, well, anyone can do that. Even I can do that with the Moroccan winning the World Cup prophecy. That's the issue when prophecies become unfalsifiable.", "What he had to do was bring the other prophets down to his level: he claimed that they also misinterpreted Revelation and made false prophecies based on that. For example, they say that chapter 11:45-46 is about a prophecy by [Nuh] that never came to pass, since his son drowned. However, if you study the verses, there is nothing there about a prophecy in the first place. Remember, a prophecy is a claim made by the prophet himself in order to prove his prophethood. This is not a prophecy.", "Another example that's brought up is the prophet, peace be upon Him, dreaming about migrating to a land with palm trees. The prophet, peace be upon Him, states that he assumed that this is referring to the [Yamama] initially. However, there's no prophecy that's made. The prophet, peace be upon Him, didn't tell his companions, \"We are going to the Yamama\", and then said, \"Oh, I misinterpreted the prophecy.\" No, in this report the prophet, peace be upon Him, is merely speaking about his own thoughts.", "Another example comes in the form of a report that we find [in] Jihad by Ibn Mubarak. We find the prophet, peace be upon him, saying, \"I saw in a dream as if Abu Jahil came to me and pledged allegiance to me.\" When it came, the Muslims said, \"This is your dream coming to reality, messenger of Allah\", for it was about Khalid's conversion. He, peace be upon him, said, \"It will be about someone else.\" Then the son of [Abujahil] converted, and that was the dream coming to reality. This also isn't a prophecy: the prophet, peace be upon him, isn't confirming who will come to Islam. Also notice he says \"it is as if\", meaning that it isn't Abujahil converting to Islam. Most importantly, this report isn't even authentic, since the narrator of the [report] isn't an eyewitness. I've actually came across [an Ahmadi] who was using this report in a quote by [Ibn Taymiyya], claiming that the prophet, peace be upon him, makes false prophecies. No, he doesn't. This is a lie against [Ibn Taymiyya]; he never said such a thing. This whole page that's about this specific report makes no claims about false prophecies in the first place.", "After the prophet, peace be upon Him, migrated to Medina, he had a dream in which he would return to Makkah and he would do tawaf, circumambulance around the Kaaba, safely. He told this to his companions, and that was a prophecy. In the fifth year after the Hijra, Muhammad, peace be upon him, and his companions went on the way to Makkah and they were stopped in Houdabia. They attempted to perform the umrah, of course; however, they weren't able to because of the polytheist Meccins. They signed the treaty and that was it. The prophet, peace be upon Him, faced with this reality, doubled down and claimed once again that they would indeed perform the [tawaf] around the [Kaaba] safely. Some Ahmedis claim that this is some sort of misinterpretation of the Revelation; however, the prophecy, after all, does not include some sort of a time limit, so there's no misinterpretation occurring in the first place.", "This sentence, \"I shall die in [Makkah or] Medina\", means that \"before my death I shall be bestowed a victory like that of Makkah, that is to say, the holy prophet, peace be upon him, had Vanquish his enemies through the manifestation of the Majestic signs of Allah, so will it happen now. The second meaning is that before my death I shall be bestowed a victory like that of Medina, which means that people's hearts will of their own be inclined towards me.\"", "First of all, why do we even accept the interpretation that's given by Mirza? At the end of the day, there is a possibility of him getting his interpretations wrong. Notice how the Revelation says that he will die in Makkah or Medina; now his interpretation says, \"I will receive Victory like that of Makkah and I'll receive Victory like that of Medina.\" So he changes the \"or\" to an \"and\". As well, there's no connection between dying and being a Victor in Mecca and Medina; it's like the opposite. His multiple prophecies kept failing one after the other, and due to this he had to shift goal posts continuously. He did this in order to make his prophecies look like they've been fulfilled. Under this framework there is no possibility of the false prophecy: you could reinterpret anything and shift goal posts all the time. Again, anyone can claim to be a prophet, and they would simply make prophecies like the one that was mentioned in the beginning.",), "Farid Responds, “If Mirza Ghulam Ahmad made a World Cup prophecy”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PKbizElwAok", ((1, "0:01–0:47"), (47, "0:47–1:30"), (103, "1:43–2:18"), (139, "2:19–2:45"), (165, "2:45–3:55"), (242, "4:02–5:12"), (316, "5:16–5:40"), (344, "5:44–6:53"),), id: 1492)
